{"type": "sena", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": "Science or Science Fiction?"}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "A Strange Call"}, {"type": "position", "content": 7}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 4}, {"type": "level", "content": "B2.3"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Exchange information about unlikely events or fairly certain conditions that will be fulfilled in present, past or future  in spoken or written texts and in work and social contexts."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": " Understands the main ideas and specific information of a complex text on both concrete and abstract topics, including technical issues in a specific field of specialization in written texts in social and work contexts."}, {"type": "secondary_2", "content": " Understands the main ideas and specific information of a complex text on both concrete and abstract topics, including technical issues in a specific field of specialization in written texts in social and work contexts."}, {"type": "secondary_3", "content": " Understands the main ideas and specific information of a complex text on both concrete and abstract topics, including technical issues in a specific field of specialization in written texts in social and work contexts."}, {"type": "secondary_4", "content": " Understands the main ideas and specific information of a complex text on both concrete and abstract topics, including technical issues in a specific field of specialization in written texts in social and work contexts."}, {"type": "secondary_5", "content": " Understands the main ideas and specific information of a complex text on both concrete and abstract topics, including technical issues in a specific field of specialization in written texts in social and work contexts."}, {"type": "secondary_6", "content": " Understands the main ideas and specific information of a complex text on both concrete and abstract topics, including technical issues in a specific field of specialization in written texts in social and work contexts."}, {"type": "secondary_7", "content": " Understands the main ideas and specific information of a complex text on both concrete and abstract topics, including technical issues in a specific field of specialization in written texts in social and work contexts."}, {"type": "secondary_8", "content": " Understands the main ideas and specific information of a complex text on both concrete and abstract topics, including technical issues in a specific field of specialization in written texts in social and work contexts."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "The Unreal Conditional in the Present and Future"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You use the Unreal Conditional to talk about events that are impossible situations in the present or events unlikely to happen in the future."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Would you be interested in documenting cases like these if it were your job?"}, {"type": "text", "content": "When you talk about unlikely or impossible situations in a present context, you use the Unreal Past Conditional."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Structure: <br> If + Past Simple / was/were/ wasn't/weren't \u2026 , would + verb"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Example: <br><span class=\"italic\">If I believed your story, I would document it and pass it along to authorities.</span> = I don't believe your story, so I'm not going to pass it along to authorities."}, {"type": "text", "content": "The if clause introduces a hypothetical situation that isn't true or is impossible. The main clause is a result of that condition, and the opposite of that situation is true."}, {"type": "text", "content": "When you talk about unlikely future situations, you use the Unreal Past in an -if clause to suggest that you are talking hypothetically and the condition may or may not be fulfilled."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Example: <br><span class=\"italic\">Would you think someone was crazy, if they told you they had? </span> =The outcome of the condition is unknown."}, {"type": "text", "content": "You also suggest that a future situation is hypothetical by using <span class=\"italic\">Suppose + Simple Past \u2026 What would you do?</span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Example: <br><span class=\"italic\">Suppose you saw a UFO. What would you do? </span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Read the following examples from the text:"}, {"type": "example", "content": "If you were to call me at the UFO sighting center, I'd ask you a lot of questions."}, {"type": "example", "content": "If I believed your story, I would document it and pass it along to authorities. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "If you heard the same story from three different people who didn't know each other, your interest would be piqued like mine was."}, {"type": "example", "content": "If you had found that none of these people had ever met would you have been surprised?"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Differences Between -ing and To-Infinitive"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "When you talk about two actions linked together, you normally use two verbs. The first verb follows the rules of conjugation for the sentence, while the second verb can be a verb in the -ing form or to+Infinitive."}, {"type": "example", "content": "We advised coming to see us."}, {"type": "text", "content": "You can use two verbs to talk about two connected actions. The main verb introduces the main action, and the second verb in the -ing form gives more information about that action."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Structure: <br>Subject + first verb + -ing form of the second verb / to+ Infinitive"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Some verbs can be followed by either an -ing verb or to+Infinitive, like <span class=\"italic\">  like, love, hate, dislike,  intend, dread, advise, recommend, regret \u2026</span> <br>The difference in meaning depends on the preceding verb."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"italic\"> Forget/Remember </span>with to+Infinitive are looking forward into a future situation. With an -ing form they refer to a past action."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"italic\"> Advise/Recommend</span> with to+Infinitive are followed by an object. With an -ing form there is no object."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"italic\"> Regret  </span>with to+Infinitive is a polite form of apology. With an -ing form it has the meaning of <span class=\"italic\">If only this hadn't happened </span>."}, {"type": "text", "content": " <span class=\"italic\">Like/dislike/love/hate/can't stand/bear </span>with to + Infinitive refer to habitual preferences."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Look at some examples from the text:"}, {"type": "example", "content": "He regretted not having a camera to capture the event. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "He forgot to look at the time until he he was back inside. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "They were shocked but remembered to stay together as the moved away. "}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "UFO "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "unidentified flying object "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Suppose you saw a UFO."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le04ob07aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use to talk about close encounters."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "flying saucer"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a UFO shaped like a disc"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example:They began to describe a \"flying saucer\" that streaked out of the sky and hovered just above them."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le04ob07aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to bathe in "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to cover in"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: The children went on to describe how the object bathed them in white light, then streaked off."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le04ob07aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to streak off"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to move away quickly"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: The children went on to describe how the object bathed them in white light, then streaked off."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le04ob07aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "disoriented "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "confused and not knowing where to go"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: He was feeling slightly disoriented."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le04ob07aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "distressed "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "upset or worried"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: He sounded clearly distressed about the incident."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le04ob07aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Go online and find accounts of UFO sightings or alien abduction on YouTube or on another site."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Listen to 3 and take notes."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Look at what you have found out and highlight things that are similar in the stories and things the are different."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Discuss your information with another student or a group and compare all 